 {"id": "REPORT-1", "title": "P1: the hourly report mislabels the actor \u2014 it calls the LLM's decisions 'JEV' and invents a ladder blocker", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 1, "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "Found by reading my own report against the run it describes. The 20:46 UTC hourly said 'JEV handled every cycle unaided' and 'JEV is deciding unaided' about run long_0927_llm2 \u2014 which is the PURE-LLM arm, where JEV is never called (decision_mode stamps 'llm', family 'system2', decision_mode_family 'system2', and escalated is False on every decision row). ROOT CAUSE, in ~/.hermes/scripts/aipp_hourly_facts.py: the script never reads decision_mode at all (0 hits) and its fields are JEV-shaped \u2014 participation_ratio is computed from 'jev_answered' (line 103) and missing classes are labelled 'top_jev_missing_classes' (line 109); 'jev_answered' is also counted up (lines 96, 141). In llm mode jev_answered is 0, so the script cannot distinguish 'System Two decided' from 'System One decided' and defaults to naming JEV. SECOND DEFECT, same report: it renders the ladder as a REQUIRED sequence \u2014 'Oak's Lab \u2014 NOT proven; goal_achieved still False because the ladder requires it'. The ladder in long_run.py:56 is a DISPLAY list; Oak's Lab is not required, and this run was seeded at Pallet Town so it could never visit the Lab. The report invented a blocker that did not exist. WHY P1: this is the surface the owner reads, and it was obscuring the exact question he was asking (which arm is doing the playing). A status surface that names the wrong actor is worse than one that says nothing. Done-when: the report reads decision_mode/decision_mode_family from the run rows and names the actual decider; ladder rows are marked proven/not-visited/not-applicable relative to the run's seed state instead of required-in-order; and a test