 {"id": "BOOT-1", "title": "P1: the long-run driver's default boot state is a DIFFERENT map position than every measured baseline", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "primary_model": null, "primary_provider": null, "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "long_run.py:47 hardcodes BOOT = data/boot.state. Probed with the project's own validator (long_run.probe_state): data/boot.state reads map 'Oak's Lab' (id 40) while data/baselines/base-1_boot.state reads 'Pallet Town' (id 0, md5 81e4ec4e8d1b62002bc78f619c5fc79c) \u2014 and Pallet Town is the state CTRL-WIN, DIST-1 and BASE-1 were all measured on. So a run launched the obvious way starts ~a whole map earlier and is silently incomparable to every number in data/baselines/, which is exactly the class of error that makes two runs look different for reasons that are not the agent. Compounding it: long_run.py passes no decision-mode flag to cron_runner.py (it invokes only --run-id, --cycles, --boot-state), so the arm a run used exists only in an ambient env var and is not recoverable from the run's own record. Worked around today by seeding long_run/<RUN_ID>_good.state from the canonical state and setting AIPP_DECISION_MODE explicitly \u2014 but that is operator discipline, not a property of the driver. Done-when: the driver takes the boot state and the decision mode as explicit arguments (or env with a loud default) and stamps BOTH into the run's status row and per-episode records, so a run cannot be launched from an unintended state or in an unrecorded arm.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T21:06:10.988705+00:00", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/ai-plays-poke-BOOT-1", "branch": "wt/BOOT-1", "worker_model": "glm-5.3-flash", "worker_provider": "xkiro", "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-09-28T12:51:43", "commit_hash": "310c5f3", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "pending CI on push (see gh run list)", "worker_summary": "Boot-sta